## R packages for survey analysis {.bkg-ship}

::::: {.columns}

:::: {.column width=49%}
::: {.fragment}
- {survey} package first on CRAN in 2003
  - descriptive analysis
  - statistical testing
  - modeling
  - weighting
  
:::
::::

::: {.column width=2%}
:::

::: {.column width=49%}
::: {.fragment}
- {srvyr} package first on CRAN in 2016
  - “wrapper” for {survey} with {tidyverse}-style syntax
  - only descriptive analysis
  
:::
::::
:::::

## Why use {srvyr} over {survey}? {.bkg-ship}

::: {.incremental}

- Use the pipe function `%>%` or `|>`
- Ability to use tidy selection
- Functions follow `snake_case` convention
:::


# Comparison of {dplyr} and {srvyr} functions {background-color='{{< brand color blue >}}' .bkg-buildings}

## Apply {dplyr} functions to survey objects {.bkg-trees}

::: {.incremental}
- A few functions in {srvyr} have counterparts in {dplyr}
  - `dplyr::summarize()` and `srvyr::summarize()`
  - `dplyr::group_by()` and `srvyr::group_by()`
- Based on the object class, R will recognize which package to use

:::

## Using `summarize()` to calculate mean and median {.bkg-serpent}

```{r}
#| label: 01-api-des-setup
#| echo: FALSE
data(api)
api_des <- apistrat |> as_survey_design(strata = stype, weights = pw)
```


::: {.panel-tabset}

### Tibble:

```{r}
#| label: 01-summarize-tib-ex
#| code-line-numbers: "|3"
towny |> 
  summarize(
    area_mean = mean(land_area_km2, na.rm=TRUE)
  )
```

### Survey object:
```{r}
#| label: 01-summarize-sv-ex
#| code-line-numbers: "|3"
api_des |> 
  summarize(
    score_mean = survey_mean(api99, na.rm=TRUE)
  )
```
:::


## Grouping  {.bkg-serpent}

::: {.panel-tabset}

### Tibble:

```{r}
#| label: 01-group-tib-ex
#| code-line-numbers: "|2"
towny |> 
  group_by(csd_type) |> 
  summarize(area_mean = mean(land_area_km2))
```

### Survey object:

```{r}
#| label: 01-group-sv-ex
#| code-line-numbers: "|2"
api_des |>
  group_by(stype) |>
  summarize(score_mean = survey_mean(api99, na.rm=TRUE))
```
:::

## Using {srvyr} functions on non-survey objects {.bkg-trees}

```{r}
#| label: 01-sv-non-svobj
#| output-location: fragment
#| error: true
towny |>
  summarize(area_mean = survey_mean(land_area_km2))
```

# European Social Survey (ESS) {background-color='{{< brand color blue >}}' .bkg-ship}

## Overview {.bkg-mountains}

- Conducted across Europe every 2 years since 2001
- Cross-sectional sample (new samples each round)
- Topics include living conditions, social structure, public opinion and attitudes
- Sampling designs vary by country but have oversight for rigor
- Most recent data available for round 11 (2023 - 2024)

## Countries for round 11

```{r}
#| label: 02-round-11-map
#| echo: false

ess_der <- read_rds(here::here("DerivedData", "ess11_der.rds"))

ess_11_countries <-
  ess_der |>
  distinct(Country, cntry)

country_shape_col <-
  World |>
  full_join(ess_11_countries, by = c("name" = "Country")) |>
  st_transform(3035) |>
  st_crop(
    st_bbox(c(xmin = -10, ymin = 32, xmax = 40, ymax = 70), crs = 4326) |>
      st_transform(3035)
  ) |>
  mutate(ESS11 = !is.na(cntry))

assign_map_colors <- function(dat_sf, idvar) {
  ids <- dat_sf[[idvar]]

  touching_polys <- tibble(
    from = ids,
    to = map(st_intersects(dat_sf), \(index) ids[index])
  ) |>
    unnest(to) |>
    filter(from < to)

  poly_graph <- igraph::graph_from_data_frame(
    touching_polys,
    directed = FALSE,
    vertices = tibble(name = ids)
  )

  colors <- igraph::greedy_vertex_coloring(
    poly_graph,
    heuristic = "colored_neighbors"
  )

  tibble(id = names(colors), ColorCat = letters[colors]) |>
    set_names(c(idvar, "ColorCat"))
}

country_shape_color <-
  country_shape_col |>
  left_join(
    assign_map_colors(country_shape_col |> filter(ESS11), "cntry"),
    by = "cntry"
  )

country_shape_col |>
  tm_shape() +
  tm_polygons(fill = "lightgrey", col = "darkgrey") +
  tm_shape(country_shape_color |> filter(ESS11)) +
  tm_polygons(
    fill = "ColorCat",
    fill.scale = tm_scale_categorical(
      values = unname(unlist(brand$color$palette))[c(5, 2, 4, 1)]
    ),
    fill.legend = tm_legend_hide()
  ) +
  tm_text("cntry") +
  tm_graticules() +
  tm_layout(
    bg.color = brand_pluck(brand, "color", "palette", "lightteal"),
    outer.bg.color = brand_pluck(brand, "color", "palette", "lightteal"),
    frame = FALSE
  )
```

## Accessing ESS data - full data

1. Create a ESS account and get your [user ID](https://ess.sikt.no/en/api){target="_blank"}

. . .

2. Open R environ file

```r
usethis::edit_r_environ()
```

. . .


3. Add your user ID to R environ

```r
ESS_USERID="12345678-1234-1234-1234-12345678900"
```

. . .

4. Restart your R session

. . .

5. Load the ESS data into R

```r
url <- glue::glue("https://api.ess.sikt.no/v1/data/dataFile/10.21338/ess11e04_2?userId={Sys.getenv('ESS_USERID')}")
ess_in <- read_parquet(url)
```

## Read data subset for this course {.bkg-serpent}

- This data is a subset of columns of full data
- Includes derived variables we created for this course
- See [data cleaning code](DataCleaning.qmd){target="_blank"}

```{r}
#| label: 02-course-dat

ess_der <- read_rds(here::here("DerivedData", "ess11_der.rds"))
```

## Why weight? Why use the design? {.bkg-trees}

- Differential selection probabilities and nonresponse are accounted for in analytic weights
- Weights are post-stratified by gender, age, education, and geographic region
- Not using weights leads to bias
- Not using design likely leads to incorrect precision, wrong degrees of freedom and not accounting for clustering

## Estimates changing with weights

```{r}
#| label: 02-why-weight
#| echo: false

ess_des <- ess_des_true <- ess_der |>
  as_survey_design(ids = psu, strata = stratum, weights = anweight, nest = TRUE)
ess_des_1 <- ess_der |>
  as_survey_design(ids = psu, strata = stratum, weights = wt1, nest = TRUE)
ess_des_wt <- ess_der |>
  as_survey_design(weights = anweight)
ess_des_srs <- ess_der |>
  as_survey_design(weights = wt1)

calc_ests <- function(des) {
  des |>
    summarize(
      .by = Country,
      p = survey_mean(
        Retired,
        proportion = TRUE,
        prop_method = "logit",
        vartype = "ci",
        na.rm = TRUE
      )
    ) |>
    rename(p_est = p)
}

allests <-
  list(a = ess_des_true, c = ess_des_wt, b = ess_des_1, d = ess_des_srs) |>
  map(calc_ests) |>
  bind_rows(.id = "method")
```

```{r}
#| label: 02-weight-compare-plot
#| echo: false
allests |>
  mutate(
    Design = recode_values(
      method,
      "a" ~ "Weights, strata, clusters",
      "b" ~ "Strata and clusters only",
      "c" ~ "Weights only",
      "d" ~ "No design"
    ) |>
      factor(
        levels = c(
          "Weights, strata, clusters",
          "Weights only",
          "Strata and clusters only",
          "No design"
        )
      )
  ) |>
  ggplot(aes(color = Design)) +
  geom_point(aes(x = Design, y = p_est)) +
  geom_errorbar(aes(x = Design, ymin = p_low, ymax = p_upp)) +
  theme_light(
    paper = brand_pluck(brand, "color", "palette", "lightteal"),
    base_family = heading_family,
    base_size = 20
  ) +
  theme(
    legend.position = "bottom",
    axis.title.x = element_blank(),
    axis.text.x = element_blank(),
    axis.ticks.x = element_blank(),
    axis.line.y = element_blank(),
    panel.grid.major.x = element_blank(),
    panel.grid.minor.x = element_blank(),
    panel.spacing = unit(0, "in")
  ) +
  facet_wrap(~Country) +
  labs(
    title = "Retirement is main activity in last 7 days, ESS 11",
    y = "Estimated proportion with 95% CI"
  ) +
  scale_y_continuous(labels = scales::percent) +
  scale_color_manual(
    values = unname(unlist(brand$color$palette[c(
      "blue",
      "teal",
      "pink",
      "purple"
    )]))
  )
```

