# Sampling designs {background-color='{{< brand color blue >}}' .bkg-buildings}

## Sampling methods {.bkg-mountains}

Units can be selected in various ways such as:

::: {.columns}

::: {.column width="33%"}
**Simple random sampling**

(with or without replacement)

Every unit has the same chance of being selected
:::

::: {.column width="33%"}
**Systematic sampling**

Sample from an ordered list, selecting individuals at an interval with a random starting point
:::

::: {.column width="33%"}
**Probability proportional to size**

Probability of selection is proportional to "size"
:::

:::

## Complex designs {.bkg-mountains}

Designs can also incorporate stratification and/or clustering:

::: {.columns}

::: {.column width="50%"}
**Stratified sampling**

Divide population into mutually exclusive subgroups (strata). Randomly sample within each stratum
:::

::: {.column width="50%"}
**Clustered sampling**

Divide population into mutually exclusive subgroups (clusters). Randomly sample clusters, then individuals within clusters
:::

:::

## Why does design matter? {.bkg-serpent}

::: {.incremental}
- The design type impacts the variability of the estimates

- Weights impact the point estimate and the variability estimates

- Specifying the components of the design (strata and/or clusters) and weights in R is necessary to get correct estimates

:::

## Determining the design {.smaller .bkg-serpent}

::: {.incremental}

- Check the documentation such as methodology, design, analysis guide, or technical documentation

- Documentation will indicate the variables needed to specify the design. Look for:
   - **Weight** (almost always)
   - **Strata and/or clusters/PSUs** (sometimes labeled pseudo-strata/pseudo-clusters) — *or*, instead, **replicate weights**
   - **Finite population correction** or population sizes (uncommon)

- Documentation may include SAS, SUDAAN, Stata and/or R syntax

:::

## Design-based vs. replicate weights {.smaller .bkg-serpent}

Two ways to tell R about a sampling design, depending on what the documentation provides:

::: {.columns}

::: {.column width="50%"}
**Strata / clusters / weights are available**

Use `as_survey_design()`
:::

::: {.column width="50%"}
**Only replicate weights are available**

Use `as_survey_rep()`
:::

:::

- Some surveys provide both; others release only replicate weights to protect respondent confidentiality
- Either path produces a `tbl_svy` object that works the same way downstream

# Specifying sampling designs in {srvyr} **without** replicate weights  {background-color='{{< brand color blue >}}' .bkg-buildings}

## Syntax for common designs: no stratification/clustering {.smaller .bkg-trees}

Simple Random Sample (SRS)

```r
apisrs %>% as_survey_design(fpc = fpc)
```

One-stage cluster sample with a FPC variable

```r
apiclus1 %>% as_survey_design(ids = dnum, weights = pw, fpc = fpc)
```

Two-stage cluster sample, weights computed from population size

```r
apiclus2 %>% as_survey_design(ids = c(dnum, snum), fpc = c(fpc1, fpc2))
```

Stratified SRS

```r
apistrat %>% as_survey_design(strata = stype, weights = pw)
```

Stratified, cluster design

```r
apistrat %>% as_survey_design(ids = dnum, strata = stype, weights = pw, nest = TRUE)
```

::: footer
Examples from `srvyr` help documentation
:::

## What does the design object look like? {.smaller .bkg-trees}

```{r}
#| label: 03-apistrat-design-demo
data(api, package = "survey")

apistrat_des <- apistrat |>
  select(cds, stype, api00, pw, fpc) |>
  as_survey_design(strata = stype, weights = pw)

apistrat_des
```

::: footer
Data: `apistrat` from the `{survey}` package
:::

# Specifying sampling designs in {srvyr} **with** replicate weights {background-color='{{< brand color blue >}}' .bkg-buildings}

## `as_survey_rep()`: syntax {.smaller .bkg-ship}

- `as_survey_rep()` creates a `tbl_svy` object that then correctly calculates weighted estimates and SEs


```{r}
#| label: 03-scd-setup
#| echo: false

data(scd, package = "survey")

scd <- scd |>
  mutate(
    rep1 = 2 * c(1, 0, 1, 0, 1, 0),
    rep2 = 2 * c(1, 0, 0, 1, 0, 1),
    rep3 = 2 * c(0, 1, 1, 0, 0, 1),
    rep4 = 2 * c(0, 1, 0, 1, 1, 0)
  )
```

```{r}
#| label: 03-scd-design-demo
scd_rep <- scd |>
  as_survey_rep(
    type = "BRR",
    repweights = starts_with("rep"),
    combined_weights = FALSE
  )

scd_rep
```

::: footer
Data: `scd` from the `{survey}` package (Levy and Lemeshow BRR example)
:::

## Recap {.bkg-left .smaller}

- Design type (SRS, stratified, clustered) shapes the variability of estimates
- Weights shape both point estimates and variance
- Two paths to a `tbl_svy` object:
  - `as_survey_design()`: strata, clusters/PSUs, weights
  - `as_survey_rep()`: replicate weights (BRR, Fay's, jackknife, bootstrap)
- Once built, the design object is ready for weighted summaries in later sections

# ESS sampling and weighting  {background-color='{{< brand color blue >}}' .bkg-buildings}

## Methodology {.bkg-buildings}

Recall, we said: "Check the documentation such as methodology, design, analysis guide, or technical documentation"

Head to the ESS website and click "Methodology": [europeansocialsurvey.org/methodology/sampling](https://www.europeansocialsurvey.org/methodology/sampling){target="_blank"}

## Sampling {.smaller .bkg-buildings}

Key sampling principles, from the ESS Sampling Guidelines:

::: {.incremental}

- Samples represent all persons aged 15+ living in private households in each country
- Individuals are selected by **strict random probability methods** at every stage — no quota sampling, no substitution of non-responders
- Each country targets a minimum "effective achieved sample size" (1,500, or 800 in countries with populations under 2 million)
- Exact designs (single-stage, stratified, multi-stage/clustered) **vary by country**, but every country's data include `psu` (primary sampling unit) and `stratum` variables

:::

::: footer
<https://www.europeansocialsurvey.org/methodology/sampling>
:::

## Weighting {.smaller .bkg-buildings}

> Four weighting variables are available: Analysis weight (anweight - combination of pspwght and pweight), Post-stratification weight (pspwght), Design weight (dweight), and Population weight (pweight).

::: {.columns}

::: {.column width="50%"}
**dweight** — corrects for unequal selection probabilities *within* a country

**pspwght** — further adjusts `dweight` for nonresponse/noncoverage using age, gender, education, region
:::

::: {.column width="50%"}
**pweight** — rescales each country to its population size, for combining countries in one analysis

**anweight** — `dweight` + `pspwght` + `pweight` combined; **recommended for all analyses**
:::

:::

::: footer
<https://www.europeansocialsurvey.org/methodology/weighting>
:::

## Weighting: the provided code {.smaller .bkg-mountains}

Clicking into the "weighting guide," we find example R code using `anweight`, the recommended weight:

```r
library(survey) # estimation

# Define sample design
design <- svydesign(ids = ~psu, strata = ~stratum, weights = ~anweight,
data = data1)
```

## First attempt {.bkg-mountains}

Using exactly the syntax from the weighting guide, we get an error:

```{r}
#| label: 03-des-error
#| error: true
svydesign(
  ids = ~ psu,
  strata = ~ stratum,
  weights = ~ anweight,
  data = ess_der
)
```

## Why the error? {.smaller .bkg-mountains}

A handful of `psu` codes repeat across different `stratum` values in the raw file:

```{r}
#| label: 03-des-why-error
ess_der |>
  distinct(cntry, psu, stratum) |>
  add_count(psu) |>
  filter(n > 1)
```

By default, `svydesign()` assumes cluster (`psu`) IDs are unique across the whole data set. `nest = TRUE` tells it to relabel clusters so they're uniquely identified *within* each stratum instead. This is the same `nest = TRUE` we saw in the stratified, cluster design example earlier.

## Applying weights, fixed {.bkg-mountains}

Including `nest = TRUE`, we get the design object:

```{r}
#| label: 03-des-survey-true
ess_des_true <- svydesign(
  ids = ~ psu,
  strata = ~ stratum,
  weights = ~ anweight,
  data = ess_der,
  nest = TRUE
)

ess_des_true
```

## From `{survey}` to `{srvyr}` {.bkg-mountains}

The same design, specified with tidy syntax:

```{r}
#| label: 03-ess-design-demo
#| output-location: slide
ess_des <- ess_der |>
  as_survey_design(
    ids = psu,
    strata = stratum,
    weights = anweight,
    nest = TRUE
  )

ess_des
```

Same result: a `tbl_svy` object ready for weighted summaries.


# Derived variables {background-color='{{< brand color blue >}}' .bkg-buildings}

## Handling missing data codes {.bkg-trees}

:::{.incremental}
- Often multiple types of missing data in surveys
    - Refused
    - Don't know
    - Valid skip
- ESS uses 7s (7,77,7777), 8s, and 9s as missing data codes
- If we leave them as is, means and statistics will be skewed incorrectly
    - Need to recode missing data values as missing (`NA`)

:::

## Recoding missing data {.bkg-trees}

```{r}
#| label: 04-derived-rename-noshow
#| echo: FALSE
ess_des_edit <- ess_des
```

```{r}
#| label: 04-derived-recodes
#| code-line-numbers: "|4,5|6-11|11|12-22|22"
#| eval: FALSE
ess_des_edit <-
  ess_des |>
  mutate(
    Happy = if_else(happy %in% c(77, 88, 99), NA, happy),
    News = if_else(nwspol %in% c(7777, 8888, 9999), NA, nwspol),
    Volunteer_Factor = case_when(
      volunfp == 1 ~ "Yes",
      volunfp == 2 ~ "No",
      TRUE ~ NA
    ) |>
      factor(levels = c("Yes", "No")),
    AgeGroup = recode_values(
      agegroup,
      1 ~ '15-24',
      2 ~ '25-34',
      3 ~ '35-44',
      4 ~ '45-54',
      5 ~ '55-64',
      6 ~ '65-74',
      7 ~ '75+'
    ) |>
      fct_reorder(agegroup)
  )

```

## Viewing recodes on design object {.smaller .bkg-serpent}

:::{.panel-tabset}

### survey_count

```{r}
#| label: 04-derived-desobj-svyct
#| code-line-numbers: "|2"
ess_des_edit |> 
  survey_count(happy,Happy)
```

### $variables
```{r}
#| label: 04-derived-desobj-svyct-vars
#| code-line-numbers: "|1|2"
ess_des_edit$variables |> 
  count(happy,Happy)
```

:::


# Descriptive analysis {background-color='{{< brand color blue >}}' .bkg-buildings}

## Common workflow {.bkg-trees}

::: {.incremental}

1. Create a `tbl_svy` object (a survey object) using: `as_survey_design()` or `as_survey_rep()`

2. Subset data (if needed) using `filter()` (to create subpopulations)

3. Specify domains of analysis using `group_by()`

4. Within `summarize()`, specify variables to calculate, including means, totals, proportions, quantiles, and more

:::

## Descriptive functions {.smaller .bkg-trees}

:::: {.columns}


::: {.column width="50%"}

Use within `summarize()`

- `survey_mean(x)`
- `survey_median(x)`
- `survey_proportion()`
- `survey_quantile(x)`
- `survey_ratio(num, den)`
- `survey_sd(x)`
- `survey_total(x)`
- `survey_var(x)`

:::


::: {.column width="50%"}

Common arguments

- `na.rm = FALSE`
- `vartype = c("se", "ci", "var", "cv")`: defaults to just "se"
- `level = 0.95`: confidence interval level of CIs 
- `deff = FALSE`: note can specify as logical OR "replace"
- `df = NULL`: degrees of freedom for CIs (defaults to `degf(design)`)

:::

::::

::: {.notes}
- survey_median/survey_quantile - no deff
- survey_sd - no deff, no measures of variation
- survey_var - no deff, no cv
:::

## Mean examples {.bkg-mountains}

::: {.panel-tabset}

### `na.rm`

```{r}
#| label: 05-mean-na
ess_des |>
  filter(cntry == "IS") |>
  summarize(
    H1 = survey_mean(News),
    H2 = survey_mean(News, na.rm = TRUE)
  )
```

### `vartype`

```{r}
#| label: 05-mean-vartype

ess_des |>
  filter(cntry == "IS") |>
  summarize(
    H1 = survey_mean(News, na.rm = TRUE, vartype = c("se", "ci", "var", "cv")),
    H2 = survey_mean(News, na.rm = TRUE, vartype = NULL)
  )
```

### `level` and `df`

```{r}
#| label: 05-mean-ci-options
ess_des |>
  filter(cntry == "IS") |>
  summarize(
    H1 = survey_mean(News, na.rm = TRUE, vartype = "ci", level = 0.95),
    H2 = survey_mean(News, na.rm = TRUE, vartype = "ci", level = 0.8),
    H3 = survey_mean(News, na.rm = TRUE, vartype = "ci", level = 0.95, df = 10)
  )
```


### `deff`

```{r}
#| label: 05-mean-deff

ess_des |>
  filter(cntry == "IS") |>
  summarize(H1 = survey_mean(News, na.rm = TRUE, deff = "replace"))
```

:::

::: {.notes}
- na.rm removes NA before calculation, similar to many other functions in R
- vartype can choose no option OR multiple
- level and df control confidence intervals
- deff is a design effect, a ratio of the variance under this design compared to a design which is SRS, >1 means less statistically efficient 
:::


## Proportion example: motivation {.bkg-mountains}

And still thinking about different ways of trying to improve things in [country] or help prevent things from going wrong, during the last 12 months, have you done any of the following? Have you volunteered for a not-for-profit or charitable organisation? (`volunfp`)

- 1: yes
- 2: no
- 7: Refusal*
- 8: Don't know*
- 9: No answer*

## Proportion example: variable coding {.bkg-mountains}

Derived variable coding options (unweighted raw data):

```{r}
#| label: 05-volunfp-code

ess_der |>
  count(
    volunfp,
    Volunteer_12,
    Volunteer_01,
    Volunteer_Factor
  )
```

## Proportion example: 2 ways {.bkg-mountains}

Two methods for proportions

1. For binary outcomes, use `survey_mean(x)`
    -  `x` is either 1/0 or TRUE/FALSE
    - can use with `across()` and several outcomes

2. Group the data by the variable (`x`) and then use `survey_prop()`
    - `x` can have 2 or more levels
    - only analyze one outcome at a time

::: aside
See [Calculating estimates for many outcomes](https://tidy-survey-r.github.io/tidy-survey-book/c05-descriptive-analysis.html#calculating-estimates-for-many-outcomes) for extending to multiple outcomes
:::

## Proportion example: 2 ways {.bkg-mountains}

::: {.panel-tabset}

### `survey_mean()`

```{r}
#| label: 05-np-survey-mean

ess_des |>
  filter(cntry == "SE") |>
  summarize(
    across(
      c(Volunteer_12, Volunteer_01),
      ~ survey_mean(.x, na.rm = TRUE)
    )
  )
```

### `survey_prop()`

```{r}
#| label: 05-np-survey-prop

ess_des |>
  filter(cntry == "SE", !is.na(Volunteer_Factor)) |>
  group_by(Volunteer_Factor) |>
  summarize(p = survey_prop())
```

:::

::: {.notes}
- survey_mean method only works for binary outcomes, bonus of being able to use across to estimate many outcomes at once
- survey_prop can be used for only one outcome at a time, can work for variables with more than 2 levels
:::

## Proportion example: arguments {.bkg-trees}

`proportion` argument in `survey_mean()`

```{r}
#| label: 05-np-proportion-arg
#| output-location: slide

ess_des |>
  filter(cntry == "SE") |>
  summarize(
    p1 = survey_mean(Volunteer_01, na.rm = TRUE, vartype = "ci"),
    p2 = survey_mean(
      Volunteer_01,
      na.rm = TRUE,
      vartype = "ci",
      proportion = TRUE
    )
  )
```


::: {.notes}
- proportion = TRUE argument only impacts confidence intervals
:::

## Proportion example: arguments {.bkg-trees}

`prop_method` argument in `survey_mean()/survey_prop()`

```{r}
#| label: 05-np-prop-method-arg
#| output-location: slide

ess_des |>
  filter(cntry == "SE", !is.na(Volunteer_Factor)) |>
  group_by(Volunteer_Factor) |>
  summarize(
    p1 = survey_prop(vartype = "ci", prop_method = "logit"),
    p2 = survey_prop(vartype = "ci", prop_method = "mean"),
    p3 = survey_prop(vartype = "ci", prop_method = "xlogit")
  ) |>
  print(width = Inf)
```

::: aside
See [`?survey::svyciprop`](https://cran.r-project.org/web/packages/survey/refman/survey.html#svyciprop) for details on implementation
:::

## Subgroup analysis

- Using `filter()` as above examples subsets data. This is done AFTER creating the design object
- Use `group_by()` to implement domain analysis

## Subgroup analysis: mean & median

- `NetUsage` is 0 if a person uses internet never, only occasionally, or a few times a week; otherwise the reported minutes using internet on a typical day

```{r}
#| label: 05-net-by-country
#| output-location: slide

ess_des |>
  group_by(Country) |>
  summarize(
    Pop = survey_total(vartype = NULL),
    NetUse_mean = survey_mean(NetUsage, na.rm = TRUE),
    NetUse_median = survey_median(NetUsage, na.rm = TRUE)
  ) |>
  gt() |>
  fmt_number(columns = starts_with("NetUse"), decimals = 2) |>
  fmt_number(columns = Pop, decimals = 0) |>
  opt_interactive()
```

## Subgroup analysis: prop., marginal {.smaller}

- `NetFreq` is how often people use the internet (categorical)
- `AgeGroup` is a categorical age variable

```{r}
#| label: 05-net-freq-age-marg
#| output-location: slide

ess_des |>
  filter(cntry == "FR", !is.na(NetFreq), !is.na(AgeGroup)) |>
  group_by(AgeGroup, NetFreq) |>
  summarize(
    N = survey_total() * 10000,
    p = survey_prop() * 100
  ) |>
  print(n = Inf)
```


## Subgroup analysis: prop., joint {.smaller}

```{r}
#| label: 05-net-freq-age-joint
#| output-location: slide

ess_des |>
  filter(cntry == "FR", !is.na(NetFreq), !is.na(AgeGroup)) |>
  group_by(interact(AgeGroup, NetFreq)) |>
  summarize(
    N = survey_total() * 10000,
    p = survey_prop() * 100
  ) |>
  print(n = Inf)
```


